  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure over the area early this morning will slide east\ntoday as low pressure tracks ENE towards the Mid Atlantic. Much\nof today will remain mostly dry with clouds increasing this\nmorning yielding mostly cloudy skies this afternoon. Latest\navailable guidance is in decent agreement that low pressure over\nthe Mid Atlantic will track ENE south of Cape Cod tonight\nthrough Monday morning. Rain will likely overspread southern NH\nthis evening and then northeastward tonight before tapering off\nNW to SE Monday morning. Models are in decent agreement that\nmeasurable rainfall will likely stay confined along and south of\nthe mountains with the latest NBM bringing QPF amounts around \n0.65 inches across southern NH decreasing to the north.\n\nAs the low exits east Monday morning skies will turn partly to\nmostly sunny across much of the area. The exception will be\nclouds across the north that will advance southward late Monday\nas a front sags south from Canada. It will be warm with highs\nranging from the low 60s north to low 70s south of the\nmountains.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe 500 mb pattern is dominated by unsettled zonal which will \npromote shortwave energy crossing the area in conjunction with \nsurface features. The first is going to be low pressure riding \nalong a frontal boundary draped over New England. This crosses \nthe area in the Tuesday/Wednesday time frame and looks to be the\nnext best chance for areawide precipitation. The latest long \nrange ensembles still have moderate uncertainty as far as QPF, \nlikely owing to there being disagreement in the track of the \nlow. The ECMWF and Canadian have the low pressure tracking \ndirectly overhead, and the GFS keeps the center north of our \narea and passing over the crown of Maine. The 25th-75th spread \nis around 0.75\" for most of the area, and a little more \nconfident in southern New Hampshire with around 0.30\" of spread.\nKeeping that in mind, current ensemble means suggest northern \nNew Hampshire and much of interior Maine, down to the Midcoast, \nhave the highest probabilities of 0.75\" to an inch of rainfall. \nCentral New Hampshire and southwestern Maine are in the 0.5 to \n0.75\" range, and southern New Hampshire is in the 0.25 to 0.50\" \nrange.\n\nDepending on how fast the low exits, rain could continue \nthrough the better part of Wednesday, but should be light as it \ntapers off. Showers are certainly going to continue in the \nmountains as northwesterly flow prevails aloft, and this could \nbring snow to higher elevations Wednesday night. The low \npressure system deepens as it moves over Atlantic Canada on \nThursday which deepens a trough off the East Coast. This keeps \nThursday dry as we see the effects of the upper ridging and high\npressure beginning to build in, but the continued cold air \nadvection aloft is going to set up several day with highs in the\n50s.\n\nRain chances return for Friday and Saturday as global models \nare in reasonable good agreement that a sharp upper level trough\nswings through somewhere in that time frame. It is too early to\ntry and flesh out details, but this is going to be another \nwindow to watch for beneficial rainfall.",
